    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full test suite is green (263 tests, 0 failures/errors).\n\n## What I did\n\nThe business-day rule lived in a single place: `NormalizeRegistrationDate.toBusinessDay(...)` in `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/NormalizeRegistrationDate.java`, which is the pipeline step that resolves the effective registration date and rolls it onto a business day (all later steps derive from its published variable).\n\nI extended it to treat public holidays like weekends:\n- Added a fixed `PUBLIC_HOLIDAYS` set: `2026-01-01`, `2026-01-26`, `2026-04-25`, `2026-12-25`, `2026-12-28`.\n- The roll-forward loop now advances a day while the date is a Saturday, Sunday, **or** a listed public holiday, so it lands on the next non-holiday business day. Because the loop keeps checking each candidate, chained cases resolve correctly too \u2014 e.g. Dec 25 (Fri, holiday) \u2192 26/27 (weekend) \u2192 Dec 28 (Mon, holiday) \u2192 Dec 29 (Tue).\n- Updated the class Javadoc to describe the new behavior.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreDefaultDateIsBusinessDay` (which asserts the invariant that the default registration date is never a weekend) passes, along with all pre-existing tests.",
